# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two
# FILES, never a shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is
# defined in run_gates.sh. Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_extractor_knex.py" "KNEX GATE: PASS" "Knex.js schema-builder extractor (.createTable/.alterTable -> table nodes + crown jewel)" "Knex.js .createTable/.createTableIfNotExists/.alterTable calls in migration files now mint table nodes and wire up the cross-substrate crown jewel: a Knex migration and a separate query file that both reference the same table name are coupled via the shared table node with NO import/call edge between them." "THE GAP FIXED: _ORM_PATTERNS covered Django/SQLAlchemy/JPA/Rails/Laravel/Go/Drizzle/Mongoose/Sequelize/EF Core/Prisma but NOT Knex.js schema builder migrations. A Knex migration file with dozens of .createTable calls yielded 0 table nodes, silencing the cross-substrate crown jewel for the entire Node.js Knex ecosystem (Directus, ApostropheCMS, Bookshelf, Objection.js, etc. -- Knex is ~3M weekly npm downloads). TWO PATTERNS ADDED to _ORM_PATTERNS in _cg_schema.py: (1) .createTable/.createTableIfNotExists -- dot-anchored so bare createTable without a chained object does not match, extracts the first string argument as the table name; (2) .alterTable -- dot-anchored, same extraction. Both patterns are precision-biased: the leading dot requirement prevents import statements, standalone function calls, comments mentioning the function name, and variable references from minting spurious table nodes. The .createTableIfNotExists variant is handled by the optional suffix group so no separate pattern is needed. WHAT THIS GATE PROVES (hermetic, no DB, no Postgres, no network): (1) knex.schema.createTable with single-quote string mints the table name; (2) schema.createTable with double-quote string also mints; (3) createTableIfNotExists mints under the same pattern; (4) .alterTable in a file under migrations/ mints the table and produces an alters edge; (5) PRECISION -- bare createTable without a leading dot (standalone call, import, comment) does NOT mint a table node; (6) CROSS-SUBSTRATE CROWN JEWEL -- a Knex migration file and a separate JS query file that reference the same table name couple via the shared table node with no import/call edge between them; (7) never-crash -- empty file and binary-ish content do not raise; (8) content-free -- migration body, column names, and comments do not appear in graph output. MEASURED on Directus (real Knex-heavy app, depth-1 clone): BEFORE 0 table nodes; AFTER 36 table nodes (all real directus_* table names from migrations -- directus_access, directus_activity, directus_collections, directus_fields, directus_files, directus_flows, directus_operations, etc.). 0 spurious names observed -- precision confirmed." 20
